  "worked_solution": "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $3$ wholes. Each whole is split into *fifths*.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/44fb9e1227b086564f4992529d6fd9308e1eb0d8b197b55e7e56afa98b8d76a4.png)\n\n \n\n- The fifths are grouped, and each group contains $\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/0abe764e73534f4ea5fe9bbe5a942fd7257a4ceca6992796c450e5760051f124.png)\n\n \n\n- There are $5$ groups in total.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/e858a032d27681d19dc411f4aa59215f8e1ac9546cdd964a017893d3004d445f.png)\n\n \n\nSo, if we divide $3$ wholes into groups of $\\frac{3}{5},$ we get $5$ groups in total.\n\nTherefore, the model represents the following division problem:\n\n$$\n3÷\\boxed{\\frac{3}{5}}=5\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\frac{3}{5}.$",
